<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parcham</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parcham ('Banner') was one of the two factions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babrak-karmal">Babrak Karmal</a>. Drawn mainly from urban, Persian-speaking intellectuals, it backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Mohammad Daoud Khan</a>'s coup of 1973, while its rival faction, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a>, was more radical. After the Soviet invasion of December 1979 Parcham held power under Karmal and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-najibullah">Mohammad Najibullah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parcham.jpg" alt="Parcham" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ericmetro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_Afghanistan_(1980-1987).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babrak-karmal">Babrak Karmal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Nur Muhammad Taraki</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</a> in 1965, and by 1967 it had split into sects, the largest of which were Parcham and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a>. The two differed in ideas and in support. The Khalqists were mainly rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a>, while the Parchamites drew on middle-class residents of the cities, most of them ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a>. Karmal held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> was not developed enough for a Leninist revolution and sought a patriotic, anti-imperialist united front instead, whereas Khalq wanted an immediate and violent overthrow of the government.</p><p>From 1968 to 1969 Parcham published a weekly magazine with the permission of King Mohammed Zahir Shah, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a> newspaper had been closed by the government in 1966. Because Parcham at first wished to keep the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, its rivals called it the Royal Communist Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Parchamites, especially in the Afghan Army, gave strong support to the 1973 coup, and many then held senior posts in the government and security forces of Mohammed Daoud Khan. Reports that Parchamis had taken part in the death of the former prime minister Mohammad Hashim Maiwandwal later led to their purge from his administration and to persecution by his regime.</p><p>The faction was reconciled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a> in 1977, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a> of 1978 many Parchamis sat in the first government. They were soon purged by Taraki's hardline leadership, which accused them of revisionism, and many were jailed and executed. Parcham came to power when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafizullah-amin">Hafizullah Amin</a> in Operation Storm-333 in December 1979. The government of Karmal struggled to win support after the excesses of the Khalqists, and the faction's own numbers had been reduced by the executions of 1978 and 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, dissatisfied with Karmal's failure to defeat the Mujahideen, saw him replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-najibullah">Mohammad Najibullah</a> in 1986. In June 1990 the Parcham-led party became the Watan (Homeland) Party, dropping every reference to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism-Leninism</a> from its manifesto in favour of an Afghan form of Islamic socialism. The Watan Party was banned on 6 May 1992. Later attempts to revive it included Mir Afghan Bawary's Watan Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and a party of the same name under General Abdul Jabar Qahraman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parcham” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hafizullah Amin killed Taraki in 1979, and in December the Soviet Union invaded, killing Amin and installing Babrak Karmal of the <b>Parcham</b> faction.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-afghanistan">Democratic Republic of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote>On 17 July 1973 the former prime minister Mohammad Daoud Khan, cousin and brother-in-law of King Zahir Shah, seized power in a bloodless coup with the support of army officers, including members of the communist <b>Parcham</b> faction.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-afghanistan-1973-1978">Republic of Afghanistan (1973-1978)</a></li></ul></section>
